<!--
  GPD Forge — the single source of truth for the product version. GPL-3.0-or-later.

  Before this file the version lived as a hand-typed literal in four independent places: the
  `/health` endpoint, `UpdateService`'s `currentVersion` DEFAULT PARAMETER, ui/package.json and
  ui/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json. Nothing kept them equal and nothing failed when they diverged.

  That is not a tidiness complaint. `UpdateService` compares the latest GitHub release against the
  version it believes is running, so a literal nobody remembers to bump makes the update checker
  answer confidently and wrongly forever — it would keep offering an update already installed. The
  same shape as every other defect this project has paid for: a value that is asserted rather than
  measured.

  So the version is declared ONCE, here, and everything else derives from it:
    - the assembly (this file feeds Version/InformationalVersion into the build),
    - `GET /version` and `GET /health`, which read the assembly rather than a literal,
    - `UpdateService`, which is handed the real running version instead of defaulting,
    - the UI and the Tauri shell, whose copies are asserted equal by VersionModelTests — the test
      fails the build when someone bumps one and forgets the others.

  Bumping a release therefore means editing this file and running that test; if the test is green,
  every surface agrees, because agreement is checked rather than assumed.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- THE version. Keep in sync with ui/package.json and ui/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json —
         VersionModelTests enforces it, so a mismatch is a failing test, not a silent drift. -->
    <GpdForgeVersion>0.1.0</GpdForgeVersion>

    <Version>$(GpdForgeVersion)</Version>
    <!-- InformationalVersion is what `GET /version` reports. .NET appends the source-revision id
         (+<commit sha>) to it when the build knows one, which is exactly the marker that would have
         made the 2026-08-28 "installed binary predates the fix" diagnosis a glance instead of an
         afternoon of string-hunting inside a shipped .exe. -->
    <InformationalVersion>$(GpdForgeVersion)</InformationalVersion>
    <Product>GPD Forge</Product>
    <Company>GPD Forge contributors</Company>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
